# Round glmref: ik's CPU node dumps of the GLM-5.3-Flash UD-Q4_K_XL split set (six shards, 199,707,321,347 B),
# each one `just dump-ref-glm5next [VARIANT]` under this card: the batch set, then step4, step4-every-node and
# d1k, one lease each. dump_ref loads without --defer-experts, so ik's loader populates every shard in turn
# (one stream), then computes on 32 threads, then writes the nodes to /root/bloomery-data (nvme1; the model is
# on nvme0). The three phases run one after the other: the wall is their sum.
# The one term the batch dump fixes: the cold-read rate of this file on this NVMe through the loader's populate.
# The file was 0.0 % resident at 2026-09-27T02:59Z (mincore over the six shards), so the first dump reads
# all of it from the device; the witness block's read-sectors and wall give the rate.
# Minutes per dump [derived]:
#   populate  cold: 199.7 GB at 1.07-1.33 GB/s, the single-stream populate rates of the 09-13 and 09-23
#             witnesses (q35oracle's 21.17 GB cold batch fits them: ~16 s): 150-187 s; at the memo's 2.5 GB/s
#             (exl3's GLM load, rig-log 09-16; parallel reads) 80 s. Warm: the page cache mapped again, >= 7 GB/s
#             (q35oracle's warm step dumps held their 21 GB inside a ~3 s fixed term): <= 29 s
#   fixed     lease, witnesses, 1,412 tensors' metadata over six shards, context: 3-15 s (q35oracle: ~3 s)
#   compute   batch and step4: 5 tokens, the memo's 1.15 TFLOP for 32 tokens scaled to 5: < 2 s.
#             d1k: 1,024 tokens at ~36 GFLOP a token (the memo's 32-token figure) = ~37 TFLOP at the 1.25 TFLOP/s
#             qwen35moe's fused 1,024-token prefill ran (4.0 s at 2.44 B active): 20-45 s
#   dump      1-6 GB a set (qwen35moe 1.5 GB; GLM's 4-wide hyper-connection residual over 46 layers) at the
#             0.5 GB/s the qwen3moe and qwen35moe step dumps wrote: 2-12 s
#   batch cold 1.4-3.6 min (1.5-3 min in the memo); step4, step4-every-node warm 0.2-0.9 min; d1k 0.6-1.6 min;
#   2.4-7.0 min in all. A step dump that reads the file from the device again (a peer's run between two dumps
#   evicted it) costs the batch's populate term again; its read-sectors say so.
kind: exclusive
reason: dump_ref runs ik on 32 CPU threads and populates the whole 199.7 GB split set into the page cache,
  evicting most of what else is cached (the V4.1 host set among it); beside a timed run or a gate batch it
  would take their cores, their memory bandwidth and their cache, so it holds the lease; why now: the
  glm5next oracle every GLM kernel and body round is judged against (round glmref, disposition G10)
minutes: 0.2..3.6
